    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is clean, additive, and closely mirrors the conventions already established in this codebase.\n\n- **Logic in the right unit.** The tier derivation stays in `OwnerMapper.membershipTier`, which already owns the BRONZE/SILVER decision \u2014 the GOLD branch is just one more guard at the top of that same small method (three cases, still very readable, not a catch-all). The counting lives in a dedicated single-purpose function step, exactly as `CountOwnerNamesakes` does for `namesakeCount`. This split (function computes a count onto the entity \u2192 mapper interprets it into a tier) is the identical pattern that already backs the SILVER rule, so a maintainer would find it immediately familiar.\n- **`CountHouseholdMembers` matches its sibling.** Same signature shape (`service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository)`), same `findAll().stream()...count()` idiom, same \"new owner not yet saved, so adjust the count\" reasoning as `CountOwnerNamesakes`. The javadoc is thorough and, like `AssignHousehold`, explicitly documents the step-ordering dependency.\n- **No awkward duplication.** It counts by the already-assigned `householdId` rather than recomputing `HouseholdKey`, which is appropriate here since `AssignHousehold` has already back-filled the shared id \u2014 keeping the two steps as distinct single responsibilities rather than overloading `AssignHousehold`. The self-exclusion guard is a trivial two-line filter consistent with the one in `AssignHousehold`.\n- **Model / schema / YAML additions** all parallel the existing `namesakeCount` / `namesake_count` field across the same four dialects and are wired into the pipeline in the natural spot (after `assignHousehold`, before downstream steps) with the same `govern: [ transaction ]` as its neighbors.\n\nNo structural changes needed.",
